  <heading level="2">Solve a Rational Equation for a Specific Variable</heading>
  <paragraph>When we solved linear equations, we learned how to solve a formula for a specific variable. Many formulas used in business, science, economics, and other fields use rational equations to model the relation between two or more variables. We will now see how to solve a rational equation for a specific variable.</paragraph>
  <paragraph>We’ll start with a formula relating distance, rate, and time. We have used it many times before, but not usually in this form.</paragraph>
  <example number="8.68">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Solve: $$\frac{D}{T} = R \text{for} T.$$</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Note any value of the variable that would make any denominator zero.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Clear the fractions by multiplying both sides of the equations by the LCD, T.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Divide both sides by R to isolate T.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
