  <paragraph>Example 8.69 uses the formula for slope that we used to get the point-slope form of an equation of a line.</paragraph>
  <example number="8.69">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Solve: $$m = \frac{x - 2}{y - 3} \text{for} y.$$</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Note any value of the variable that would make any denominator zero.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Clear the fractions by multiplying both sides of the equations by the LCD, $$y - 3$$ .</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Isolate the term with y.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Divide both sides by m to isolate y.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="8.137">
