    <paragraph>Solve: $$x = \frac{y}{1 - y}$$ for $$y.$$</paragraph>
  </note>
  <paragraph>Be sure to follow all the steps in Example 8.70. It may look like a very simple formula, but we cannot solve it instantly for either denominator.</paragraph>
  <example number="8.70">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Solve $$\frac{1}{c} + \frac{1}{m} = 1 \text{for} c.$$</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Note any value of the variable that would make any denominator zero.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Clear the fractions by multiplying both sides of the equations by the LCD, $$cm$$ .</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Distribute.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Collect the terms with c to the right.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Factor the expression on the right.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>To isolate c, divide both sides by $$m - 1$$ .</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify by removing common factors.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <paragraph>Notice that even though we excluded $$c = 0 \text{and} m = 0$$ from the original equation, we must also now state that $$m \neq 1$$ .</paragraph>
    </solution>
  </example>
